WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.790
Film Courage: With ESCAPE ROOM, you wrote
the script, did you also pitch yourself as

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.390
being the director or was this something that
came later?

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.580
Peter Dukes, Filmmaker: Right off the bat,
I always knew I wanted to write and direct.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.040
I had done that with all of my short films.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.219
But directing in the feature world is very
tricky.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:18.980
Investors like their pool of people.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:23.749
Unless you have a theatrically distributed
feature film under your belt and named talent,

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:26.269
you are usually not going to get on their
list.

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:30.919
Even if you’ve done amazing shorts, it’s
just not going to happen.

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:35.580
I won’t say that is every investor out there
but just in my experience I’ve had people

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.910
say “Hey, we love your reels.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.130
We love your short films.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:40.130
It’s not going to happen.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:41.340
Hope you understand?”

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.210
It’s your money, of course I understand.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:48.350
You need to work with people you are confident
can handle a feature.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:53.870
So I knew I needed to put myself in a position
where I had some leverage if I was ever going

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.320
to direct one of my own scripts we’ll say.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.060
So ESCAPE ROOM comes around and the guys loved
the script.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.680
One of the producers said “I want to direct
it.”

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:04.900
And I said “No deal.”

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.509
I direct it or it’s no deal.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:10.659
I gambled and I was willing to gamble.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:14.950
Just like gambling with money in Las Vegas,
if it’s money that you can afford to lose,

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.979
then gamble it.

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:18.180
Same thing with film.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.799
I was willing to lose the deal over it.

00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:26.220
It wasn’t a bluff because I knew that was
my best shot in years to actually direct a

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.479
feature and if I passed it up and just sold
them the script, I’m back playing the game

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:32.479
I’ve been playing for years.

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:36.659
I was trying to get myself in a position to
do it again, so I gambled.

00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:39.189
“It’s me or its a no-go.”

00:01:39.189 --> 00:01:43.799
And he kind of looked back down at the table
for a minute and then he looked back up and

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:47.049
he goes “Okay.”

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:48.341
So I said “Great.”

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:54.159
He had to take me to eventually what would
be the production company and the sales agents

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:55.770
in one.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.130
They had to sign off on me also.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.350
I had one meeting with them and I had been
doing shorts for years so I knew what I was

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.350
talking about.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:07.320
I wasn’t trying to bluff my way into directing
a feature, I knew it was of a scale that I

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.280
could handle, I wasn’t in over my head,
answered all of their questions and was confident,

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.810
all of the things you should be in a business
meeting.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:23.220
And after one fairly brief meeting, the agreement
was made and we were off and ready to go.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.350
Not every script I write I would make the
same gamble, I would make the same moves.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:32.510
Some of my scripts are not passion projects,
I’m just writing them to be sold.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.310
But I do have some that I would say the same
thing again and I wouldn’t be bluffing,

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:40.790
it was me or I’ll keep it on the shelf.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:46.150
Not everyone will be forced to be in that
position where if I want to direct this, if

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.400
I want to direct this, I’ll max out my credit
cards.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.319
If they won’t give it to me, I’ll just
do it myself.

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:58.530
So luckily I didn’t have to go down that
route but anyways I engineered a situation

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:05.060
where not force my way in but use what little
leverage I had to my advantage and luckily

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.060
it panned out.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.680
Film Courage: Where did you film ESCAPE ROOM?

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.439
Peter: By and large it was shot in Burbank
(local).

00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:20.560
We had a good portion of the film I wanted
to write something that could be done relatively

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.090
inexpensive so a big portion of the film is
inside an escape room.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:32.060
They built one on a stage in a building, they
built it on a camera house that was still

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.790
open for business (it was a camera rental
house is what it was).

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.810
We had a little stage and Skeet Ulrich and
all of these guys in the hallways out there

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.349
also in a building that was open for business.

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:46.959
So people would be leaving their office and
walk through the shot.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.519
It was incredible, I wasn’t prepared for
that.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:50.819
You just roll with it, what are you going
to do?.

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:54.950
It was this building right next to the Burbank
Airport, so we had planes flying over all

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.950
of the time ruining our shot, so we had to
deal with that also.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:02.340
Then we had some desert shots that were shot
out in Lancaster [California] and some other

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:07.109
various locations but by and large it was
local because it was all designed to be.

00:04:07.109 --> 00:04:12.000
I wanted to write something that relatively
speaking was just the easiest thing to produce.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.349
What’s the most attractive thing I can make
for investors?

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:19.440
This is something that can be shot for real
quick for very little money, high concept,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.030
good hook, why wouldn’t we produce it?

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.830
I was just trying to give them everything
you could give them and reduce the amount

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:28.080
of no’s that they could possibly come up
with.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.980
Film Courage: That’s pretty cool.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.400
See it’s like these green lights that just
happen for you.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.190
Peter: Yes…and I didn’t know what would
happen after that.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:40.160
I was like here I am, I’ve been working
all of my life and I’ve got a movie playing

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.160
in theaters?

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.520
I was like “This is great.”

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.770
I was like it’s a small movie so I don’t
know what attention I’m going to get?

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:58.650
As I mentioned before, no agency called, no
management company called.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.509
I did get press but not like The Hollywood
Reporter.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:06.069
It didn’t really do that much for me so
I didn’t know what to expect.

00:05:06.069 --> 00:05:07.669
As I said, the fight never ends.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:12.440
I’ve got a bunch of feature deals that are
pending right now.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.759
They are in pre-production, they are in development,
one is locking up distribution.

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:19.240
Not one of them stemmed from ESCAPE ROOM.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.660
Those were other deals where I kept up the
fight, kept out there going just grinding

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.400
away.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.440
So it’s an interesting profession that we’ve
choice, we never know what you’re going

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.860
to get.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.470
As I’ve said, I’m even keel about it.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:41.810
I’m always working on new projects and I’ll
never stop and I’m not surprised that ESCAPE

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:45.510
ROOM didn’t open up the flood gates for
me and I’m actually okay with it.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.819
You kind of just keep going.

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:51.710
Every day you keep going and I’ve been doing
it for so many years now that it is all I

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:52.949
know.

00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:58.729
But all the more reason for beginner directors
to prepare themselves for that.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:03.479
The fight is kind of endless, it’s kind
of forever.

00:06:03.479 --> 00:06:07.349
And I think for most of us (for me), the fight
is part of what we love about the business

00:06:07.349 --> 00:06:11.520
because not all of us can do what it is that
we do.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.870
To me it’s kind of a privilege to be able
to say that not only do you survive the business,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:17.870
but you thrive in it.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:21.439
So you just kind of grasp onto the positive
aspects of it and keep going.

